News Analysis System Tutorial
*****************************

The News Analysis System keeps an argument map of a news story. For each
question a story raises, it tells you whether the evidence public at a
given moment supports an answer, what that answer rests on, and what new
evidence would change it. This tutorial teaches the ideas from the
ground up, then shows you how to write a case of your own.

Review status: this tutorial was drafted by Claude (Anthropic) at Andrew
Dougherty's direction on 14 October 2026. Every command output shown was
checked against the running engine. Andrew has looked it over but has
not yet verified it.

* Menu:

* Overview::            What the system does, in one page.
* Installing::          Installing the packages and checking them.
* First Steps::         Running the demo and asking questions.
* Core Ideas::          Evidence, rules, arguments, attacks, verdicts.
* A Case From Scratch:: Building a small case step by step.
* Commands::            Every colloquy command, with examples.
* The Demo Cases::      What each of the three cases teaches.
* Experiments::         Changing cases and watching verdicts move.
* Robustness::          Conspiracy numbers and priority sensitivity.
* Case File Reference:: Every predicate a case file can use.
* How It Works::        The engine's algorithm, step by step.
* Limitations::         What the engine does not do yet.


