1 Overview
**********

A news story is a set of claims that arrive over time. Early claims
often come from whoever had the most immediate access, and later
evidence can overturn them. Readers rarely go back and recheck what they
believed once that happens.

The system does the rechecking for you. You record each piece of
evidence with its source and the moment it became public, and you write
rules that say how evidence supports conclusions. The engine,
'colloquy', then works out which conclusions stand, at any moment you
choose.

It never gives a probability. Every verdict is one of five statuses:

ACCEPTED
     Some argument for the claim survives every attack.

REFUTED
     Some argument for a contrary claim is accepted.

DEFEATED
     Arguments for the claim exist, but none survives. This means _not
     established_, which is different from shown false.

CONTESTED
     Arguments for and against defeat each other and neither side wins.

-
     Nothing supports the claim yet.

Because every verdict comes with the arguments behind it, you can always
ask _why_, and you can always ask _what would change it_. Those two
questions are the point of the system.


