3 First Steps
*************

Run the whole demo first, to see what output looks like:

     news-analysis-demo | less

Now ask a question yourself. The fake Pentagon image case covers one
morning in May 2023. Ask for the state of play at 10:06, when the image
first spread:

     colloquy status pentagon-fake-image --at 2023-05-22T10:06

You will see:

       ACCEPTED   explosion_at_pentagon
                  Was there an explosion at the Pentagon?
       ACCEPTED   image_is_authentic
                  Is the viral image a real photograph of the scene?
       -          markets_reacted_to_claim
                  Did markets react to the claim?

At 10:06, with only the image public, the engine accepts the explosion.
That is not a bug. Nothing yet contradicts the image, so on the evidence
available it stands. Now ask again without '--at', which means "as of
the latest evidence":

     colloquy status pentagon-fake-image

Both claims are now REFUTED, and the market reaction is ACCEPTED: the
markets did move, even though the event did not happen.

Two more questions are worth trying straight away:

     colloquy whatif pentagon-fake-image explosion_at_pentagon \
       --at 2023-05-22T10:06
     colloquy explain pentagon-fake-image explosion_at_pentagon

The first lists what a careful reader should have asked at 10:06. The
second shows, step by step, why the explosion claim is refuted now.


