5 A Case From Scratch
*********************

This chapter builds a small fictional case, the Riverside Bridge rumour.
The finished file is installed as
'/usr/share/doc/news-analysis-system/examples/bridge-rumour.pl' (in the
source tree, 'doc/examples/bridge-rumour.pl'); you will write it
yourself here.

5.1 Step 1: sources and the helper
==================================

Create '~/bridge.pl' and start with the title and the sources:

     case_title('Tutorial: the Riverside Bridge rumour (fictional)').

     src(post,  'A social media post (fictional)',              'none').
     src(city,  'City traffic department account (fictional)', 'none').
     src(check, 'Reverse image search result (fictional)',      'none').
     src(tv,    'Local TV live broadcast (fictional)',          'none').

     premise(Id, L, [date(D), source(S), url(U), prio(P), note(N)|X]) :-
         p(Id, L, D, K, P, N, X),
         src(K, S, U).

The 'premise' clause is a helper: it lets you write each piece of
evidence as one short 'p/7' fact and attaches the source for you. Copy
it unchanged into every case.

5.2 Step 2: the questions
=========================

Say which claims the case is about:

     topic(bridge_collapsed, 'Did the Riverside Bridge collapse?').
     topic(photo_is_current, 'Is the viral photo from today?').

5.3 Step 3: the first piece of evidence and its rules
=====================================================

     p(photo, viral_photo_of_collapsed_bridge, '2026-03-01T08:00', post, 1,
       'A post shows a collapsed bridge captioned "Riverside Bridge is gone"',
       []).

     defeasible(r_photo_current, [viral_photo_of_collapsed_bridge],
                photo_is_current,
                [prio(1), label('a photo posted as news is presumed to be from today')]).
     critical_question(r_photo_current, 'Has the photo appeared anywhere before?').

     defeasible(r_photo_shows, [viral_photo_of_collapsed_bridge, photo_is_current],
                bridge_collapsed,
                [prio(1), label('a current photo shows what happened')]).

The arguments of 'p/7' are: an identifier, the literal, the time, the
source key, the priority, a one-line note for the timeline, and a list
of extras (use '[]').

Run it:

     colloquy status ~/bridge.pl

Both topics are ACCEPTED. With one photo and nothing else, the engine
has no reason to doubt it. Ask what would change that:

     colloquy whatif ~/bridge.pl bridge_collapsed

It lists the photo premise, both rules, and the critical question you
attached. This list is the reader's homework at 08:00.

5.4 Step 4: the authority speaks
================================

Add the city's statement and a rule for it:

     p(city, city_says_bridge_open, '2026-03-01T08:30', city, 3,
       'City traffic department: bridge open, a minor accident was cleared',
       []).

     defeasible(r_city, [city_says_bridge_open], neg(bridge_collapsed),
                [prio(3), label('the authority responsible for the bridge reports it open')]).

Run 'colloquy timeline ~/bridge.pl'. At 08:30, 'bridge_collapsed' goes
from ACCEPTED to REFUTED: a priority-3 rebut beats a priority-1
argument. But 'photo_is_current' is still ACCEPTED, because nothing has
attacked that part yet.

5.5 Step 5: the photo is old
============================

     p(search, photo_found_online_in_2019, '2026-03-01T08:45', check, 3,
       'The same photo appears online in 2019, from a different country',
       []).

     defeasible(r_old_photo, [photo_found_online_in_2019], neg(photo_is_current),
                [prio(3), label('a photo published in 2019 is not from today')]).

Now at 08:45 'photo_is_current' is REFUTED too. The critical question
from step 3 has been answered.

5.6 Step 6: evidence that changes nothing
=========================================

     p(live, live_tv_shows_traffic_on_bridge, '2026-03-01T09:00', tv, 4,
       'Live TV shows traffic crossing the bridge',
       []).

     defeasible(r_live, [live_tv_shows_traffic_on_bridge], neg(bridge_collapsed),
                [prio(4), label('a live broadcast shows the bridge standing')]).

The timeline at 09:00 says '(no change in any conclusion)'. The claim
was already refuted; the live broadcast is a second, independent reason.
'colloquy explain ~/bridge.pl bridge_collapsed' now shows two accepted
arguments against the rumour.

5.7 What you built
==================

     2026-03-01T08:00   bridge_collapsed: - -> ACCEPTED
                        photo_is_current: - -> ACCEPTED
     2026-03-01T08:30   bridge_collapsed: ACCEPTED -> REFUTED
     2026-03-01T08:45   photo_is_current: ACCEPTED -> REFUTED
     2026-03-01T09:00   (no change in any conclusion)

That is the whole method. Real cases differ only in having more
evidence, more careful rules, and real sources with URLs.


