6 Commands
**********

Every command takes a CASE: either a file path, or the name of an
installed case such as 'opera-neutrinos'. Most take '--at DATE', where
DATE is 'YYYY-MM-DD' or 'YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM'. Without it, the engine uses
the latest evidence in the case.

'colloquy status CASE [--at DATE]'
     The status of every topic in the case.

'colloquy explain CASE LITERAL [--at DATE]'
     Every argument for LITERAL, labelled IN, OUT or UNDECIDED, with its
     premises and sources, and the arguments that defeat it.

'colloquy whatif CASE LITERAL [--at DATE]'
     For an accepted claim: the premises, rules and critical questions
     whose defeat would change it. For a refuted claim: the points in
     the winning argument that would have to fall. For a claim with no
     argument: which rules could conclude it.

'colloquy timeline CASE'
     Steps through each moment at which evidence arrived, listing the
     new evidence and every status that changed.

'colloquy robustness CASE [--at DATE] [--max N]'
     For each topic, the conspiracy number and, for a topic not
     accepted, how many sources would have to be set aside for it to
     become accepted; then every verdict that changes if one priority
     moves by one. *Note Robustness::.

'colloquy aif CASE [--at DATE]'
     Writes the argument graph as AIF JSON, the Argument Interchange
     Format used by other argumentation tools.

'colloquy cases'
     Lists the installed cases. Set 'COLLOQUY_CASE_PATH' to a
     colon-separated list of directories to add your own.

'news-analysis-demo'
     Replays all three demo cases and shows one 'whatif' and one
     'explain'.

Literals are written as Prolog terms. Put quotes around any that contain
parentheses, for example "neg(bridge_collapsed)".


