7 The Demo Cases
****************

The three installed cases were chosen to end three different ways.

7.1 pentagon-fake-image: refuted within the day
===============================================

On 22 May 2023 an apparently AI-generated image of smoke "at the
Pentagon" spread on Twitter at 10:06, and US stocks fell sharply for a
few minutes. The Arlington County fire department and the Pentagon Force
Protection Agency said there was no incident, and an analyst at
Bellingcat pointed out artifacts in the image and the absence of any
other witnesses.

What to look at: 'colloquy whatif' at 10:06 lists the three questions
the afternoon's evidence answered. The market reaction stays ACCEPTED
throughout: it shows belief, not truth.

7.2 opera-neutrinos: contested, then self-corrected
===================================================

In September 2011 the OPERA experiment reported neutrinos arriving about
60 ns earlier than light would. The engine holds this as CONTESTED
against special relativity, rather than accepting or dismissing it. A
November re-test on the same apparatus changes nothing. In February 2012
the team found a loose fibre-optic link and a fast-running clock, and
the claim becomes REFUTED.

What to look at: the timeline after February 2012. An independent
replication, the resignations of the experiment's leaders, and the final
corrected results all change nothing. The first is redundant; the second
is not evidence; the third confirms what was already known.

7.3 yandex-datacentres-bots: not established (live)
===================================================

In October 2026 Ukrainian drones struck Yandex data centres, and claims
spread that pro-Kremlin bot activity on Western social media fell
afterwards. The engine accepts that the strikes happened (both sides
describe them) and that automated traffic from Yandex's network fell. It
finds the claim that political bot activity fell DEFEATED: the testimony
offered cites no data, the user reports are unmeasured, and the traffic
figure also counts legitimate crawlers. A popular version naming
pro-Trump bots specifically has no argument at all, because no source
checked makes that claim.

What to look at: 'colloquy explain yandex-datacentres-bots
russian_political_bot_activity_fell'. Three arguments, each undercut for
a different reason.

This case is live. As new evidence appears, add it and rerun the
timeline.


