8 Experiments
*************

8.1 Experiment 1: what evidence would establish the bot claim?
==============================================================

  1. Copy the case:

          cp /usr/share/news-analysis-system/cases/yandex-datacentres-bots.pl \
             ~/yandex-test.pl

  2. Append a hypothetical independent study:

          cat >> ~/yandex-test.pl <<'EOF'

          src(hyp, 'HYPOTHETICAL evidence for experimenting', 'none').
          p(study, study_measures_political_bot_drop, '2026-10-20', hyp, 4,
            'HYPOTHETICAL: an independent study measures a drop in bot posts', []).
          defeasible(r_study, [study_measures_political_bot_drop],
                     russian_political_bot_activity_fell,
                     [prio(4), scheme(measurement), label('an independent measurement')]).
          EOF

  3. Replay it with 'colloquy timeline ~/yandex-test.pl'. It worked if
     2026-10-20 shows 'russian_political_bot_activity_fell: DEFEATED ->
     ACCEPTED'. The causal claim becomes ACCEPTED too, because its other
     premise (the strikes) was already established.

The lesson: more testimony would not change the verdict. Measurement
would.

8.2 Experiment 2: how much do priorities matter?
================================================

  1. Copy the OPERA case and lower the theory's priority from 3 to 2:

          cp /usr/share/news-analysis-system/cases/opera-neutrinos.pl ~/opera-test.pl
          sed -i 's/\[prio(3), scheme(established_theory)/[prio(2), scheme(established_theory)/' \
              ~/opera-test.pl

  2. Run 'colloquy timeline ~/opera-test.pl'. It worked if 2011-09-23
     now shows ACCEPTED instead of CONTESTED.

One number changed the verdict. This is why every priority in a case
should be visible and open to challenge, and why the cases use a
published scale rather than numbers chosen per claim.

8.3 Experiment 3: an unfalsifiable claim
========================================

Add a premise and a rule to any copied case that conclude a new topic,
without any evidence that could ever count against it. Then run
'colloquy whatif' on it. Notice that the only attack points are the
premise itself and the rule. If no conceivable observation could
undermine the premise or undercut the rule, the claim can never be
tested, and the engine's verdict on it says nothing about the world.
That is the reason for the critical question "what observation would
count against this?".


