9 Robustness
************

A verdict is only as strong as what it rests on. The 'robustness'
command asks two questions of every topic.

9.1 Conspiracy numbers
======================

The "conspiracy number" of a claim is the smallest number of sources
that would have to be wrong, mistaken or colluding for its status to
change. The engine finds it by setting sources aside, one, then two,
then three, and recomputing. The name comes from McAllester's conspiracy
numbers for game-tree search, which count how many leaves must change to
change the value at the root.

     colloquy robustness pentagon-fake-image

     explosion_at_pentagon  REFUTED
       conspiracy number 1: set aside [official] and it becomes DEFEATED
       to become ACCEPTED: set aside 3: [analysis,impostor,official]

Read it like this. If the officials' statement were wrong, the claim of
an explosion would no longer be refuted, but it would still not be
established (DEFEATED), because the image analysis would still sink the
photograph. For the explosion to be _accepted_, three separate sources
would all have to be wrong: the officials, the image analyst, and the
record of who spread the image.

The measure treats both directions alike. A claim that one interested
party is lying has a conspiracy number of one, and suspecting it is
reasonable. A claim that needs many independent sources to be
coordinated has a high number, and the engine says so without calling it
absurd.

Two results in the demo cases are worth noticing:

   * In the Yandex case, 'russian_political_bot_activity_fell' becomes
     ACCEPTED if one source is set aside: the caveat that the traffic
     figure also counts crawlers. The claim is one fact away from
     established, and that fact is the thing to check.

   * 'pro_trump_bots_specifically_reduced' cannot become accepted by
     setting any sources aside. Nothing supports it; only new evidence
     could.

By default each premise is its own source. When several premises come
from one origin (one outlet repeating another, or one person quoted in
several places), add 'origin(O)' to their metadata and they count once.

9.2 Priority sensitivity
========================

The second part of the report moves each priority up and down by one and
lists every verdict that changes. On the latest evidence the demo cases
are stable: no single move changes anything. Earlier moments can be
fragile:

     colloquy robustness opera-neutrinos --at 2011-09-23

     Priority sensitivity at 2011-09-23 (each priority moved by one):
       r_measure 3 -> 2: neutrinos_faster_than_light CONTESTED -> REFUTED
       r_measure 3 -> 4: neutrinos_faster_than_light CONTESTED -> ACCEPTED
       r_theory 3 -> 2: neutrinos_faster_than_light CONTESTED -> ACCEPTED
       r_theory 3 -> 4: neutrinos_faster_than_light CONTESTED -> REFUTED

In September 2011 the verdict rested entirely on judging the measurement
and the theory equally reliable. That is honest, and the report makes
the dependence visible: anyone who disputes the verdict can see exactly
which judgement to argue about.


