10 Case File Reference
**********************

A case file is ordinary Prolog, loaded into its own module. These are
the predicates the engine reads:

'case_title(TEXT)'
     The title printed by 'status' and 'timeline'.

'premise(ID, LITERAL, META)'
     An attackable statement of record. META is a list that may contain
     'date(D)', 'source(S)', 'url(U)', 'prio(P)', 'note(N)',
     'precision(P)' and 'origin(O)'. A premise with no date is available
     at every moment; use that for background knowledge. Premises with
     the same 'origin' count as one source for conspiracy numbers.

'axiom(ID, LITERAL, META)'
     A statement that cannot be attacked. Use very sparingly.

'defeasible(ID, ANTECEDENTS, CONCLUSION, META)'
     A defeasible rule. META may contain 'prio(P)', 'scheme(S)' and
     'label(Text)'.

'strict(ID, ANTECEDENTS, CONCLUSION)'
     A strict rule.

'critical_question(RULEID, TEXT)'
     A question a reader should ask before trusting the rule. Shown by
     'whatif'.

'contrary(A, B)'
     A and B cannot both hold. Symmetric.

'topic(LITERAL, DESCRIPTION)'
     A question the case tracks. If a case declares no topics, the
     engine uses every defeasible conclusion.

Dates are atoms: "2026-10-12" or "2026-10-12T14:30". When a source gives
no time for a statement, use the time the source was published and add
'precision(upper_bound)': the statement was public by then, possibly
earlier. Use 'precision(approximate)' or 'precision(month)' where those
apply.


